{# ── Chapter: Hook ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="hook">
  <h2>The orchestrator that routed a refund to an agent that couldn't do refunds</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      A team built a dashboard that split customer requests across three backend agents — one for scheduling callbacks, one for account lookups, one labeled "Billing Agent" for anything money-related. The routing logic was simple: if the request mentioned "refund" or "charge," send it to the Billing Agent. No check of what that agent could actually do — just a name that sounded right.
    </p>
    <p>
      The Billing Agent's real capabilities only covered read-only invoice lookups. It had no refund-processing skill at all — that lived on a different agent the dashboard hadn't even wired up yet. The refund request got sent anyway, the agent declined it, and the dashboard just showed "Submitted" — because nothing in the routing step had ever asked the agent what it could do before handing it the task.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      Under the A2A (Agent2Agent) protocol, every compliant agent is expected to be interrogated before it's trusted with a task — it publishes an Agent Card describing exactly what it supports. An orchestrator that routes by name convention instead of reading that card has no way to know it guessed wrong until the agent's own response says so, and not every agent responds with an error a UI bothers to surface. This one just silently declined, and the UI had nothing better to show than "Submitted."
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      This is a discovery problem, not a routing-logic problem — "route billing-shaped requests to the agent named Billing" is a reasonable rule given the information it chose to use. The bug is treating an agent's name as its interface, when A2A explicitly hands you the real interface as structured data: the Agent Card's <code>skills</code> array and <code>securitySchemes</code> object are meant to be fetched and matched against a task before a single request goes out, not inferred from branding or prior assumption.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Chapter: Concept ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="concept">
  <h2>Fetch the Agent Card before you trust the agent</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      The Agent Card is a JSON document every A2A-compliant agent publishes at a fixed, predictable address on its own domain: <code>/.well-known/agent-card.json</code> — the same "well-known URI" pattern the web already uses for things like <code>/.well-known/security.txt</code>. Before an orchestrator routes a task to an agent, it fetches that file and reads what's actually in it: the agent's name, the specific skills it declares, and what it needs for authentication.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      Concretely: a <code>GET</code> to <code>https://{agent-domain}/.well-known/agent-card.json</code> returns an Agent Card with a <code>skills</code> array of named, described capabilities (each with its own id, so "refund-processing" and "invoice-lookup" are distinguishable, not lumped under one vague "billing" label) plus a <code>securitySchemes</code> object listing what auth it accepts — API key, OAuth2, HTTP bearer token. A task should only be routed to an agent whose skills list contains something that actually matches what's being asked.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      Two checks matter here, and they're independent. Skill match: does the card's <code>skills</code> array contain something matching the task. Auth match: does the orchestrator hold credentials satisfying one of the card's declared <code>securitySchemes</code>. A card check that only inspects skills and ignores security schemes will still send unroutable requests — they'll just fail on a 401 instead of "skill not found." The card also separately declares <code>capabilities</code> (streaming, push notifications), which govern how you talk to an agent once you've routed to it, not whether you should route there — don't collapse that into the same check.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Chapter: Story ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="story-1">
  <h2>A support-ops team's three-agent dashboard</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      The team behind that dashboard eventually pulled the routing logic apart to find out why refunds kept silently failing. The problem wasn't a bug in the refund logic — there wasn't any refund logic in the Billing Agent at all. Its Agent Card, if anyone had fetched it, declared exactly one skill: <code>invoice-lookup</code>, read-only. Refund processing existed on a completely separate agent that the dashboard had never been told about.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      The fix wasn't just "connect the right agent" — it was adding a discovery step at all. On startup, the dashboard now fetches every connected agent's Agent Card and caches its declared skills and auth scheme. When a request comes in, routing checks that cache first: does any connected agent's <code>skills</code> list contain something matching this task, and does the orchestrator hold credentials for that agent's declared scheme. Only then does a request go out.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      They changed the UI alongside the routing logic, not just the logic itself — instead of a bare "Submitted," the dashboard now shows why a task went where it went: "Routed to Refunds Agent — skill <code>refund-processing</code> matched, auth: Bearer token accepted." That mattered as much as the discovery step: discovery without visibility just moves the guessing from the orchestrator's code into a debugging session six weeks later, when someone's trying to figure out why a different task went to the wrong place.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

