{# ── Chapter: FE Depth ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="fe-depth">
  <h2>Cache the card, but don't trust it forever</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      Fetching every agent's card on every single task would be slow, so orchestrators usually cache the result after the first fetch. That's fine, but it means the cache can go stale — an agent's actual capabilities can change after you've cached an old version of its card, so a orchestrator that never re-checks can end up making the exact same wrong-guess mistake, just against cached data instead of a name string.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      A reasonable middle ground: cache the card with a short TTL, and always re-fetch (or at least re-validate) before routing anything security-sensitive, even if the skill-match cache is still considered fresh. Skill lists change slowly in practice; auth requirements are the kind of thing that changes on a deploy and should be checked more defensively.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      Also don't assume a card fetch itself is trustworthy without thinking about it — you're pulling JSON from a domain you don't control and using it to decide what credentials to send. Treat the fetch like any other cross-origin resource: verify you're talking to the domain you expect (no silent redirect to somewhere else), and don't blindly forward every credential type you hold just because a card claims to accept it. Discovery reduces the guessing problem; it doesn't remove the need to still validate what you're trusting.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

